{"content":"Applied fix to sqlparse/filters/reindent.py `_split_statements`: it now only inserts a newline before a new DML/DDL statement token if the preceding text doesn't already end with a newline/carriage return (mirroring the same guard already used in `_split_kwds`). Previously it unconditionally inserted a newline, which combined with the newline later added in `process()` (based on whether `_last_stmt` already ends with '\n') caused duplicate/extra blank lines for the 'stmts' reindent case (e.g. 'select foo; select bar' -> 'select foo;\n\nselect bar' is now correct and stable, whereas before the fix, related multi-statement/idempotency scenarios produced inconsistent extra newlines). Verified with multiple manual checks:
- format('select foo; select bar', reindent=True) == 'select foo;\n\nselect bar'
- format('select foo; -- test\nselect bar', reindent=True) == 'select foo; -- test\n\nselect bar'
- BEGIN/END block and parenthesis/subquery reindent cases still work as before (unaffected by the change since it only skips redundant newline insertion when one is already present).
No test suite is present in this checkout to run the hidden upstream tests against, so verification was done via direct sqlparse.format() calls reproducing the reported regression scenario.","tool_calls":[{"id":"final_1","name":"run","arguments":{"command":"python -c "import sqlparse
